Example 1 – simple multiplication
  Calculate &frac{2}{5} × &frac{3}{4}
  Multiply numerators: 2 × 3 = 6
  Multiply denominators: 5 × 4 = 20
  &frac{6}{20} = &frac{3}{10}
  = &frac{3}{10}
  (Always simplify your final answer.)

Example 2 – cross-cancelling
  Calculate &frac{3}{8} × &frac{4}{9}
  Cancel 3 and 9 (common factor 3): &frac{1}{8} × &frac{4}{3}
  Cancel 4 and 8 (common factor 4): &frac{1}{2} × &frac{1}{3}
  = &frac{1}{6}
  = &frac{1}{6}
  (Cross-cancelling makes the multiplication simpler.)

✓ &frac{2}{3} × &frac{4}{5} = &frac{8}{15}
✗ &frac{2}{3} × &frac{4}{5} = &frac{6}{8}
✓ You can cross-cancel before multiplying
✗ Multiplying fractions always gives a bigger number
✓ 1&frac{1}{2} × &frac{2}{3} = 1

